--TEST--
`Core\Request::bodyStream` names the walk rather than taking it, so a program answering no request is refused where it asked and not at the first chunk — spec § 15, `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways`, `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Where the refusal lands is the question, and it is one only a lazy member can
// get wrong: a `foreach` drives `iterate()`, `advance()` and `current()` after
// the member has already answered, so a `bodyStream` that deferred its check to
// the first pull would hand a program a walk it could never take. The loop body
// below is unreachable either way -- what the case pins is that nothing inside
// it ran, and that the sentence names `bodyStream` and not one of the three
// names a `foreach` drives.
try {
    foreach (Core\Request::bodyStream() as tainted bytes $chunk) {
        echo "walked a chunk\n";
    }
    echo "the walk ended without a refusal\n";
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Request::bodyStream(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
